## Architecture

{{ package_name }} is a command-line kernel. It owns almost no features itself: it boots,
loads a list of addons, resolves a command string to a Python function, and runs it.
Everything a user actually invokes lives in an addon.

### What this repository contains

Very little. Three Python files:

- `{{ path("__main__.py") }}` — instantiates the kernel and calls `exec()`
- `{{ path("src/common/wex.py") }}` — the `Wex` class, which extends the kernel and
  declares the addon list
- `{{ path("src/workdir/wex_workdir.py") }}` — the workdir class for this repository itself

The rest is shell entry points in `{{ path("bin") }}`, configuration in `{{ path(".wex") }}`,
and the declaration of every addon as a dependency in `{{ path("pyproject.toml") }}`.
No command is implemented here.

### Boot sequence

`{{ path("bin/wex") }}` is a shell wrapper that runs `{{ path("__main__.py") }}`, which
does one thing:

```python
Wex(entrypoint_path=__file__).exec()
```

`Wex.exec()` imports every addon manager and hands the list to `Kernel.setup()`, which
runs its initialisation steps in a fixed order:

```
_init_local_env             load .wex/local/env.yml into the environment
_init_command_line_kernel
_init_logging
_init_addons                instantiate the addon managers
_auto_detect_env            detect and persist the env vars addons declare
_init_resolvers             collect the command resolvers addons contribute
_init_runners               Python and YAML command runners
_init_middlewares
_init_registry              build or load the command registry
_init_script_runner_registry
_init_step_guard_registry
```

The order matters: addons are instantiated before resolvers, middlewares and the
registry, because each of those is assembled from what the addons declare.

`exec_argv()` then turns the command-line arguments into `CommandRequest` objects and
executes them.

### Addons

There is no automatic discovery. The addon list is hard-coded in `Wex.exec()`, so the
set of available features is fixed at build time and readable in one place.

An addon manager extends `AbstractAddonManager` and contributes some of:

| Method | Contributes |
| --- | --- |
| `get_package_module()` | its Python package root — the only abstract method |
| `get_command_resolver_classes()` | additional command resolvers |
| `get_middlewares_classes()` | request-processing middlewares |
| `get_local_configurable_keys()` | environment variables to detect and persist |
| `get_workdir_types()` | workdir classes, keyed by type name |
| `get_step_guard_classes()` | guards for YAML workflow steps |
| `get_webhook_resolvers()` | webhook entry points |

Commands themselves are never declared: an addon holds a `commands/` directory, and the
resolver scans it.

### Command resolution

A command string is matched against four patterns, each handled by its own resolver:

| Form | Example | Resolved from |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `addon::group/command` | `app::state/rectify` | the addon's `commands/` directory |
| `.group/command` | `.deploy/staging` | `.wex/commands/` of the current project |
| `~group/command` | `~notes/open` | `~/.wex/commands/` |
| `@service::group/command` | `@mysql::db/dump` | the service addon |

The addon prefix is optional in the first form: `state/rectify` works when the group and
command name are unambiguous.

`CommandAddress` is the pivot between representations. Its three components map directly
onto a Python function name, joined by double underscores, and onto a file path:

```
app::state/rectify   →   app__state__rectify   →   commands/state/rectify.py
```

That convention is what lets a resolver find a function by scanning a directory, with no
registration step anywhere.

Resolvers are either static or live. Addon commands are static — scanned once, then
cached in `.wex/tmp/registry.json`. Project and user commands are live: re-scanned at
every boot, so a file you just created is immediately callable.

### The kernel and its collaborators

| Subsystem | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `io` | all terminal output, indentation, width — an `IoManager` from `wexample-prompt` |
| `workdir` | the filesystem structure as a declarative tree, from `wexample-filestate` |
| `registry` | flattened command metadata: address, aliases, tags, sudo requirement |
| `resolvers` | command string to Python function |
| `runners` | execution of the resolved function, Python or YAML workflow |
| `middlewares` | layers wrapping request processing, contributed by addons |

### Where the boundary is

Two packages sit under the kernel, and the split is deliberate.

`wexample-app` is generic: `AbstractKernel`, `CommandRequest`, `CommandRunner`,
`AbstractCommandResolver`, option parsing, output handlers. Nothing in it knows what wex
is; it would serve any Python CLI application.

`wexample-wex-core` adds what makes wex wex: the addon manager framework, the
`addon::group/name` convention and its resolvers, command attachments
(before / after / always_after), sudo enforcement, YAML workflows and step guards.

Below both sit the shared libraries — `wexample-helpers`, `wexample-config`,
`wexample-filestate`, `wexample-cli`, `wexample-prompt` — which know nothing of wex at
all.
